  "answer_text": "Staring at a pregnancy test and trying to decode the results can bring up a wide range of emotions\u2014whether you are hoping for a positive, hoping for a negative, or just feeling anxious. \n\nHome pregnancy tests work by detecting a hormone called **hCG** (human chorionic gonadotropin), which your body begins producing after a fertilized egg implants in the uterus. \n\n---\n\n### 1. How to Read Your Results\nAlways refer to the specific instruction booklet that came with your test, as different brands use different symbols. Generally, results fall into three categories:\n\n*   **Positive Result:** \n    *   **What it looks like:** Two lines (even if one is very light), a plus sign ($+$), or the word \"Pregnant\" / \"Yes\" on a digital screen.\n    *   **Rule of thumb:** If a line has **visible color** (pink or blue) and appears within the time frame specified in the instructions (usually 3 to 10 minutes), **it is a positive result**. The thickness or darkness of the line does not matter; any colored line means hCG was detected.\n*   **Negative Result:** \n    *   **What it looks like:** Only one line (the \"control\" line, which proves the test is working), a minus sign ($-$), or the word \"Not Pregnant\" / \"No\".\n*   **Invalid Result:** \n    *   **What it looks like:** No lines appear at all, or only the test line appears without the control line. This means the test is defective or was not saturated with enough urine. You will need to discard it and try again with a new test.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Faint Line Dilemma: Faint Positive vs. Evaporation Line\nOne of the most common sources of confusion is a barely-visible line. It is highly useful to know the difference between an early positive and an \"evaporation line\":\n\n| Feature | Faint Positive | Evaporation (Evap) Line |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **What it means** | You are pregnant (very early stages). | The test is negative. It is just a dry urine watermark. |\n| **Color** | Has distinct **pink or blue** pigment. | Colorless, **grey, white, or shadow-like**. |\n| **Timing** | Appears within the test's window (usually 3\u20135 minutes). | Appears *after* the test has dried (usually 10+ minutes). |\n\n**Why do faint lines happen?** \nIf it is a true faint positive, it usually means you tested very early in your pregnancy when hCG levels are still quite low, or your urine was diluted. \n\n---\n\n### 3. Tips for the Most Accurate Result\nTo avoid confusing \"ghost lines\" or false results, keep these testing tips in mind:\n*   **Use first-morning urine:** Your urine is most concentrated when you first wake up, containing the highest levels of hCG. If you test later in the day, try not to drink excess fluids for a few hours beforehand so you don't dilute the sample.\n*   **Set a timer:** Do not guess the timing. Read the test exactly when the instructions tell you to. \n*   **Throw it away after the window:** Do not dig a test out of the trash hours later to look at it again. As the urine dries on the test strip, evaporation lines *will* form and make a negative test look positive.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What to Do Next\n\n*   **If your test is Positive:** \n    Contact an obstetrician, gynecologist, or healthcare provider to schedule your first prenatal appointment (usually set for around 8 weeks after your last period). In the meantime, start taking a daily prenatal vitamin with folic acid, and avoid alcohol, smoking, and certain medications.\n*   **If your test is Negative (but your period is late):** \n    It is possible you ovulated later than you thought, and your body hasn't produced enough hCG to register on a test yet. Wait **48 to 72 hours** and test again. hCG levels double roughly every two days in early pregnancy, so if you are pregnant, a clearer line should show up soon. \n*   **If you are still unsure:** \n    Consider buying a **digital pregnancy test**, which spells out \"Pregnant\" or \"Not Pregnant\" to eliminate any guesswork about faint lines. Alternatively, your healthcare provider can perform a highly sensitive blood test to confirm.",
